rtune, I say nothing further of the latter, but of the former, desiring to receive some assurance, I say that, although when I wrote I offered myself with all my heart to Your Paternity, now more than ever I offer myself for every part of the Indies, and chiefly for those where most is suffered; and I supplicate Your Paternity to be pleased to fulfill this my desire, born of the will I have to do penance for my sins, by endeavoring to make up for the negligence I have had in my own salvation by diligence in that of others. Which I hope to obtain from Your Paternity, for the love of God, I prepare myself for every occasion of departure, assisted by the holy sacrifices of Your Paternity."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 80,
    "year": 1633,
    "date": "May 17, 1633",
    "place": "Milan",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 739, 242",
    "excerpt": "Your Paternity having, to my petition to go to the Indies, already twice answered that there was no occasion, since at that time there was no thought of sending laborers to those parts; I supplicate Your Paternity now that the occasion offers itself, to deign to satisfy this my desire, with which I entered the Company, in the hope that it should once be fulfilled. True it is that I recognize in myself, not even from afar, that heap of virtues which Your Paternity says in one of yours is necessary to such laborers; nevertheless I hope in the grace of the Lord that, as by His goodness He has hitherto maintained in me the vocation to the Indies, so He may also give me sufficient strength to correspond to it, whenever it pleases Him to inspire Your Paternity to give me, with the leave, your blessing, which prostrate at your feet, as an unworthy son, I ask in your holy sacrifices.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "Your Paternity having, to my petition to go to the Indies, already twice answered that there was no occasion, since at that time there was no thought of sending laborers to those parts; I supplicate Your Paternity now that the occasion offers itself, to deign to satisfy this my desire, with which I entered the Company, in the hope that it should once be fulfilled. True it is that I recognize in myself, not even from afar, that heap of virtues which Your Paternity says in one of yours is necessary to such laborers; nevertheless I hope in the grace of the Lord that, as by His goodness He has hitherto maintained in me the vocation to the Indies, so He may also give me sufficient strength to correspond to it, whenever it pleases Him to inspire Your Paternity to give me, with the leave, your blessing, which prostrate at your feet, as an unworthy son, I ask in your holy sacrifices."
    ]
