 {
  "id": "hist-86",
  "fictional": false,
  "name": "Fortunato Serafini",
  "author_raw": "Serafini, Fortunato",
  "age": null,
  "yearsInSociety": null,
  "formation": null,
  "studies": null,
  "skills": [],
  "health": null,
  "assignment": null,
  "destination": "east-indies",
  "dossier": "Wrote 2 letters from Rome (Collegio Germanico), place not stated (1636–1643) petitioning the General for the foreign missions; requested destinations: East Indies (1), Aethiopia (1).",
  "letters": [
   {
    "n": 86,
    "year": 1636,
    "date": "April 20, 1636",
    "place": null,
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 740, 432",
    "excerpt": "After having had recourse to Your Paternity many times in person, and also with written letters, being unable now without great difficulty to come in person to set forth, or to confirm, my ancient desire, given me by the Lord God together with that of the religious life, to leave this world wholly, that I may carry out the Lord's will — one might say in another world — if with nothing else, at least with a prompt obedience and an entire oblation of my own life, however it is to be employed, I come with this letter to supplicate anew with all affection that Your Paternity would cooperate with the divine will, which I have experienced with so many and such certain demonstrations that nothing is lacking but the approbation of Your Paternity — an infallible proof, indeed a most evident sign, of it.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "After having had recourse to Your Paternity many times in person, and also with written letters, being unable now without great difficulty to come in person to set forth, or to confirm, my ancient desire, given me by the Lord God together with that of the religious life, to leave this world wholly, that I may carry out the Lord's will — one might say in another world — if with nothing else, at least with a prompt obedience and an entire oblation of my own life, however it is to be employed, I come with this letter to supplicate anew with all affection that Your Paternity would cooperate with the divine will, which I have experienced with so many and such certain demonstrations that nothing is lacking but the approbation of Your Paternity — an infallible proof, indeed a most evident sign, of it. I wrote at length two years ago, in a Latin letter of mine, the motives and progress of this vocation of mine, so it is not necessary to repeat them; and even so, that I may not further occupy Your Paternity, occupied as you are with so many other things, I add only that daily the affection toward that place warms and inflames ever more, and the Indies stand almost continually impressed upon my heart, in which I dwell often in thought; and I find peace in no other end than in a death so many times imagined, and as many times almost promised and accepted. I have made the Company's ordinary experiments of the pilgrimage, the hospital, Christian doctrine; I have tried also some little mission in the squares of Rome and at Frascati, as a prelude to the great ones; and for all this I am neither dead nor even fallen ill, and I find myself now in the seminary in the best of health, by the grace of God, so that I see there can be no impediment. I was moved to make this new instance by seeing my fellow disciples of fewer years in religion, but of truly greater spirit, sent, while I remain here. May the Holy Spirit move the heart of Your Paternity, as I pray and hope, and I make you profound reverence."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 161,
    "year": 1643,
    "date": "July 11, 1643",
    "place": "Rome (Collegio Germanico)",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 744, 111",
    "excerpt": "To compress into little the much that I must most humbly lay before Your Paternity concerning my vocation to the Indies, I say with all truth that it has lasted the space of twelve continuous years that I have unworthily spent in the Religious state, and has not only never grown lukewarm but has ever been more inflamed, especially on occasions of retreat, whether for the Spiritual Exercises or for the renewal of vows, and at many other times kindled by unusual impulses.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "To compress into little the much that I must most humbly lay before Your Paternity concerning my vocation to the Indies, I say with all truth that it has lasted the space of twelve continuous years that I have unworthily spent in the Religious state, and has not only never grown lukewarm but has ever been more inflamed, especially on occasions of retreat, whether for the Spiritual Exercises or for the renewal of vows, and at many other times kindled by unusual impulses. To this is added that I have weighed well all the difficulties that might draw me back, but they have served only to confirm me all the more in my resolve — indeed, in the vow lately made with Your Paternity's approval — to seek with the greatest insistence I can the mission to the Indies, where I may scatter, amid a thousand labors in the service of souls, the Gospel and my blood; of which I feel intensely assured, with particular consolation and certain hope of obtaining the grace from Your Paternity in the present occasion of the coming of the Father Procurator of Ethiopia, to whom I have offered myself, and with whom I recognize it to be God's will that I go, without further delay, under pretext of which I shall never allow myself to be induced to desist from the insistence which I feel in obligation to make, that I may correspond to the divine vocation, seeing clearly that by delaying I would run a manifest danger of failing God, not without great reproach on the Day of Judgment; besides which I am so far advanced in age and in my studies that any delay seems to me to be nothing but very harmful to me. In sum, my principal motive, which counts for a thousand, is to do the will of God, of which, as I have never doubted in all this time, I am likewise persuaded that Your Paternity is not to have any doubt; and therefore, setting aside every other consideration to the contrary, I am to be granted the so-much-desired and so-often-requested license, which now anew I request with the last effort of affection and with greater insistence than all those past, so that, fully assured of God's will, I may with all confidence in His help set myself to an enterprise so arduous and so disproportionate to my weakness and unfitness, which is to cooperate in the salvation of so many souls bought with the precious blood of our Lord Jesus Christ. I add no more, for I deem that this is enough to move Your Paternity to cooperate in the most singular grace that God does me, by sending me where He calls me, I promising with His grace never to turn back but to go ever forward unto death, and death of the Cross, for which in conclusion I beg Your Paternity to help me with your Holy Sacrifices and prayers, to which with all my heart I commend myself."
    ]
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "id": "hist-89",
  "fictional": false,
  "name": "Mario Finechiaro",
  "author_raw": "Finechiaro, Mario",
  "age": null,
  "yearsInSociety": null,
  "formation": null,
  "studies": null,
  "skills": [],
  "health": null,
  "assignment": null,
  "destination": "east-indies",
  "dossier": "Wrote 12 letters from Caltanissetta, Mineo, Palermo (1637–1642) petitioning the General for the foreign missions; requested destinations: East Indies (5), Japan (5), China (2).",
  "letters": [
   {
    "n": 89,
    "year": 1637,
    "date": "June 8, 1637",
    "place": "Caltanissetta",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 741, 27",
    "excerpt": "It is a wonder with what joys my soul has been inflamed since you recently, by your letters, stirred in me the expectation of an Indian expedition; but while this care sweetly wastes and turns me, as to what year you will at last relegate me, I would very much like to know. For why should I deny it? A flame divinely kindled in me since I was a little boy so rouses me day and night that I plainly waste away with desire; whence, if I learn that I have been drawn far off from you for a long time, I shall judge my life altogether bitter; and indeed I cannot but be tormented when an ardor so lofty in its conception, strengthened by the age of so many years, falls nearly to nothing (for I understand it is put off from day to day).",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "It is a wonder with what joys my soul has been inflamed since you recently, by your letters, stirred in me the expectation of an Indian expedition; but while this care sweetly wastes and turns me, as to what year you will at last relegate me, I would very much like to know. For why should I deny it? A flame divinely kindled in me since I was a little boy so rouses me day and night that I plainly waste away with desire; whence, if I learn that I have been drawn far off from you for a long time, I shall judge my life altogether bitter; and indeed I cannot but be tormented when an ardor so lofty in its conception, strengthened by the age of so many years, falls nearly to nothing (for I understand it is put off from day to day). But if my letter has perhaps slipped out over-boldly, may you, my good and easy Father, grant pardon to my ambition, and lend me, I pray, that hand which alone can heap upon me the fullness of joys, whose image, wandering even in my dreams, so tickles me with delight that every moment seems slow to me. But since I know your will to be well disposed toward the zeal of your sons, there is no reason for me to add a word; nay, if in your most limpid conferences with God you regard me, I trust that I shall obtain what I desire. Farewell."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 91,
    "year": 1637,
    "date": "November 12, 1637",
    "place": "Caltanissetta",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 741, 67",
    "excerpt": "Since the flames of my ardor for the Indian enterprise are doubled in me more and more each day, I feel myself sweetly and unceasingly pricked to write to You again with greater care; but what argument will a frail page with faltering little letters set before You from a mind covered, indeed almost wholly consumed, by the little flames of heaven? Yet I will not suffer my spirit to despair; for that most sweet patronage of the Mother of God, through which I understand all my concerns to be sustained, will at once receive my cause with kindly brow, she who strikes into me even now, by her present deity, a desire so great that I plainly leap for joy and am carried aloft by divine longings.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "Since the flames of my ardor for the Indian enterprise are doubled in me more and more each day, I feel myself sweetly and unceasingly pricked to write to You again with greater care; but what argument will a frail page with faltering little letters set before You from a mind covered, indeed almost wholly consumed, by the little flames of heaven? Yet I will not suffer my spirit to despair; for that most sweet patronage of the Mother of God, through which I understand all my concerns to be sustained, will at once receive my cause with kindly brow, she who strikes into me even now, by her present deity, a desire so great that I plainly leap for joy and am carried aloft by divine longings. For although You keep me as Your intermediary in every place, yet set beside the Indian regions all things grow cheap to me; for my spirit, even from far away, disports itself among them as in its own lakes: and if, with no interval of time intervening, You will open to me the ready entrance that is shut, and suffer me to commit myself to the way, I shall straightway be overwhelmed with the most vehement joys.",
     "Wherefore, before the cares of good letters grow cold in me, I contend the more keenly with You, Father, to answer my wishes — I whose ears and spirit are raised in expectation of it. I will not have You send back any letter to me unless it be brimming with rich hope, a first swallow announcing that You are most ready to fulfill my vows: and if You will comply the sooner, a heap of joy will befall me; but if otherwise than I desire should befall, I shall be tormented with unceasing longings. Finally, since I rightly know that You wish me well for my own sake, I shall proceed no further: only that You promote my vows, whole and entire, whence I confidently trust to gather a full harvest of my desire."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 94,
    "year": 1638,
    "date": "June 9, 1638",
    "place": "Palermo",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 742, 52",
    "excerpt": "As great as is my consolation at hearing of the happy lot of those Fathers, presently called by Your Paternity to so blessed an enterprise, so much the more is the grief quickened in my soul at finding myself left out of such an election, so long sighed for by me; wherefore I can do nothing else but weep for my faults, which doubtless accuse me before the Divine countenance as most unworthy of so abundant a grace as the Mission of the Indies and suffering for Christ. Yet I trust that the Most Blessed Virgin, as she once was pleased to kindle in me with so fervent an impulse this flame, will finally deign in her mercy to bring it to the desired end; praying also warmly Your Paternity to show me at least some small ray of hope with a brief line of extraordinary consolation — grieving as I do beyond measure that I cannot communicate to you fully with the pen the burning desire that my anxious soul feels in every part.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "As great as is my consolation at hearing of the happy lot of those Fathers, presently called by Your Paternity to so blessed an enterprise, so much the more is the grief quickened in my soul at finding myself left out of such an election, so long sighed for by me; wherefore I can do nothing else but weep for my faults, which doubtless accuse me before the Divine countenance as most unworthy of so abundant a grace as the Mission of the Indies and suffering for Christ. Yet I trust that the Most Blessed Virgin, as she once was pleased to kindle in me with so fervent an impulse this flame, will finally deign in her mercy to bring it to the desired end; praying also warmly Your Paternity to show me at least some small ray of hope with a brief line of extraordinary consolation — grieving as I do beyond measure that I cannot communicate to you fully with the pen the burning desire that my anxious soul feels in every part. With which I commend myself to you in the Holy Sacrifices."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 95,
    "year": 1638,
    "date": "June 9, 1638",
    "place": "Mineo",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 741, 128",
    "excerpt": "The heavenly flame cannot be hidden, nor can one act so that its burning is not felt; hence I cannot but trouble Your Paternity with repeated letters, You who with a nod can calm it. My Father, the desire that the Lord kindled in me for the Mission of the Indies has grown so great that I hope the same Lord, with His Most Holy Mother, will move Your Paternity to console me; for the works of God are most perfect, and I feel myself urged to entreat you of it as much as I fear that, once the course of philosophy has begun next year, my desire, already ripe and brooking no more delay, may be prolonged for me.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "The heavenly flame cannot be hidden, nor can one act so that its burning is not felt; hence I cannot but trouble Your Paternity with repeated letters, You who with a nod can calm it. My Father, the desire that the Lord kindled in me for the Mission of the Indies has grown so great that I hope the same Lord, with His Most Holy Mother, will move Your Paternity to console me; for the works of God are most perfect, and I feel myself urged to entreat you of it as much as I fear that, once the course of philosophy has begun next year, my desire, already ripe and brooking no more delay, may be prolonged for me. I pray you, therefore, that with the cool of the season the flame of my long affection may likewise be lightened; and at least, should Your Paternity command otherwise, do not delay this answer, so that with the hope of receiving it happily I may go on feeding and beguiling my desire. With which I offer myself to you, commending myself to the Holy Sacrifices and Prayers."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 99,
    "year": 1638,
    "date": "December 19, 1638",
    "place": "Palermo",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 741, 289",
    "excerpt": "I had hoped by this hour to have begun, with the others, the so-longed-for Mission of the Indies, when I find myself unexpectedly shut out of so happy an election: and what most afflicts my soul is the doubt that such an opportunity will not offer itself again for the present, as this year's has to so many blessed Fathers and brothers; so that I confess to Your Paternity that, thinking on it, I cannot but weep for my failings, which doubtless have kept me from heaven's so-desired contentment; for in such wise has the desire been kindled day by day that no consolation can satisfy it save that which you alone with a nod can grant me; nor do I believe that the begun course of philosophy will restrain the charity of Your Paternity from fulfilling my desires to the full — which would indeed be to heap still more grief upon a soul so long anxious.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "I had hoped by this hour to have begun, with the others, the so-longed-for Mission of the Indies, when I find myself unexpectedly shut out of so happy an election: and what most afflicts my soul is the doubt that such an opportunity will not offer itself again for the present, as this year's has to so many blessed Fathers and brothers; so that I confess to Your Paternity that, thinking on it, I cannot but weep for my failings, which doubtless have kept me from heaven's so-desired contentment; for in such wise has the desire been kindled day by day that no consolation can satisfy it save that which you alone with a nod can grant me; nor do I believe that the begun course of philosophy will restrain the charity of Your Paternity from fulfilling my desires to the full — which would indeed be to heap still more grief upon a soul so long anxious. With which I commend myself to you in the Holy Sacrifices."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 102,
    "year": 1639,
    "date": "October 16, 1639",
    "place": "Palermo",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 742, 168",
    "excerpt": "I do not know what more to take hold of in order to console in some part my soul, so greatly afflicted by the solicitude and anxiety of bringing to completion the ancient desire for the Mission of Japan; knowing especially that, although my desire grows and kindles more from hour to hour, the Lord does not deign to show me in Your Paternity's letters any ray of full and extraordinary hope. Often thinking upon this, I cannot but dissolve into most copious tears, attempting by this means to wash away altogether the filth of my sins, to which I attribute the long prolongation of the final execution of the said Mission.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "I do not know what more to take hold of in order to console in some part my soul, so greatly afflicted by the solicitude and anxiety of bringing to completion the ancient desire for the Mission of Japan; knowing especially that, although my desire grows and kindles more from hour to hour, the Lord does not deign to show me in Your Paternity's letters any ray of full and extraordinary hope. Often thinking upon this, I cannot but dissolve into most copious tears, attempting by this means to wash away altogether the filth of my sins, to which I attribute the long prolongation of the final execution of the said Mission. I console myself, on the other hand, by considering the fount whence I know so divine an inspiration to have derived to me; for I trust that the Most Holy Mother, who of her mere benignity deigned to transfuse into me so vehement a zeal for the conversion of that remote people, will also take it upon her to satisfy me altogether and to temper the flame of desire that continually consumes me — not ceasing also to beg Your Paternity to have compassion on a son of yours in Christ in a matter so pious, since with a single nod, if it please you, you could free me from so many anxieties and pains that bind me in such a manner. Nothing further occurring, I end only desiring that you consider this desire to be extraordinary, nor do I know how to console myself: wherewith I commend myself to your Holy Sacrifices and prayers."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 107,
    "year": 1640,
    "date": "January 29, 1640",
    "place": "Palermo",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 742, 233",
    "excerpt": "I wrote to Your Paternity last October recalling my ancient desire for the Mission of Japan; and no answer having come to hand, I write to you anew briefly, praying you earnestly to condescend to the longing of an unworthy son of yours in so just and pious a request. There is no need here to repeat, as I have always done since my Novitiate, the state of my said Vocation; it suffices that Your Paternity know only that every delay I accept as chastisement from the Lord in punishment of my sins. I therefore ask anew of Your Paternity, with all the affection of my heart, that you would finally consent to the sentiment, so kindled and strong, which from Heaven comes to me more firmly reconfirmed each day; for if you will, I do not believe a readier occasion could offer itself than the present one, which we are advised of from China by one of our Sicilian Fathers — namely, the coming of Father Alvaro Semedo, Procurator of that same China, to bring with him into those parts 100 subjects.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "I wrote to Your Paternity last October recalling my ancient desire for the Mission of Japan; and no answer having come to hand, I write to you anew briefly, praying you earnestly to condescend to the longing of an unworthy son of yours in so just and pious a request. There is no need here to repeat, as I have always done since my Novitiate, the state of my said Vocation; it suffices that Your Paternity know only that every delay I accept as chastisement from the Lord in punishment of my sins. I therefore ask anew of Your Paternity, with all the affection of my heart, that you would finally consent to the sentiment, so kindled and strong, which from Heaven comes to me more firmly reconfirmed each day; for if you will, I do not believe a readier occasion could offer itself than the present one, which we are advised of from China by one of our Sicilian Fathers — namely, the coming of Father Alvaro Semedo, Procurator of that same China, to bring with him into those parts 100 subjects. I would go on further, but I end, seized by impatient grief, for it seemed to me that I could not, nor knew how to, explain my desire to Your Paternity — perhaps for this reason it has not been granted. I pray the Lord that He deign to be my interpreter before Your Paternity, to whom I shall remain with immortal gratitude if ever I am consoled by you. Wherewith I offer myself to you most resignedly, commending myself to your Holy Sacrifices and Prayers."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 116,
    "year": 1640,
    "date": "May 27, 1640",
    "place": "Palermo",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 742, 288",
    "excerpt": "I write anew to Your Paternity concerning my Vocation to Japan, beseeching him with the most heartfelt affection of my soul to have compassion on a son of his in Christ in so pious a matter; and let him know that I shall never be forgetful of so great a benefit, for me unique and singular. And though the cause may perhaps be the lack of opportunity — the Procurator of China not having come — yet I cannot be quieted, knowing that Your Paternity, this notwithstanding, can console me, as he has just now done with certain others; for which I cannot but afflict myself with holy envy.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "I write anew to Your Paternity concerning my Vocation to Japan, beseeching him with the most heartfelt affection of my soul to have compassion on a son of his in Christ in so pious a matter; and let him know that I shall never be forgetful of so great a benefit, for me unique and singular. And though the cause may perhaps be the lack of opportunity — the Procurator of China not having come — yet I cannot be quieted, knowing that Your Paternity, this notwithstanding, can console me, as he has just now done with certain others; for which I cannot but afflict myself with holy envy. Of grace, let him console me, for I am all too distressed by it, feeling myself inwardly reproved as of little worth and unmeriting. At least let him promise me that among the first who shall be called, I too shall be; for I know no other remedy to quiet myself a little than this. Father, as I write this, tears of tenderness come to me, nor do I know how to express my heart to him; and this has become for me a solicitude and a most sorrowful anxiety. I end, to trouble him no further, only reminding him that I am utterly, utterly disconsolate and afflicted in my heart, thinking of nothing else but this; beseeching the Most Blessed Virgin that she would deign at last to move Your Paternity to my just prayers; and offering myself to him most ready, I commend myself in his most holy Sacrifices and Prayers."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 123,
    "year": 1640,
    "date": "September 9, 1640",
    "place": "Palermo",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 742, 372",
    "excerpt": "I am aware that it is not necessary to commend to Your Paternity so often my desire for the Japan mission, believing that you cannot so easily have forgotten my long insistence and fervor: yet today I feel myself inwardly moved to lay before you the state of this vocation of mine, shown to you so many, many times; knowing especially that if Your Paternity will take pity on this your most unworthy son in Christ, you can console him — for among so many, I too, poor wretch, might well find a place.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "I am aware that it is not necessary to commend to Your Paternity so often my desire for the Japan mission, believing that you cannot so easily have forgotten my long insistence and fervor: yet today I feel myself inwardly moved to lay before you the state of this vocation of mine, shown to you so many, many times; knowing especially that if Your Paternity will take pity on this your most unworthy son in Christ, you can console him — for among so many, I too, poor wretch, might well find a place. So I pray you in visceribus Iesu Christi (in the heart of Jesus Christ) to console me at least by giving me sure hope, that I may in part mitigate the ardor; which has grown so keen from day to day that, knowing not what else to do, I have promised it by vow to the Divine Majesty, with the permission of my Superiors; whence, seeing that it is not yet answered, I feel myself afflicted, and often cannot restrain myself from breaking out in copious weeping. I finish, lest I weary you, and offering myself to you, commend myself in your holy sacrifices."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 125,
    "year": 1640,
    "date": "October 1, 1640",
    "place": "Palermo",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 742, 383",
    "excerpt": "I think that by now, by God’s grace, the Father Procurator of China has arrived there; wherefore I pray with the warmest affection of my heart Your Paternity to remember this your unworthy son in Christ.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "I think that by now, by God’s grace, the Father Procurator of China has arrived there; wherefore I pray with the warmest affection of my heart Your Paternity to remember this your unworthy son in Christ. Our Father, now is the time at last to console me, and among so many I too may well find a place — you know how long I have desired and sighed for this; and I have taken such affection to this mission of Japan that I would have all ask for it, and would desire all to employ themselves wholly in aid of those poor souls redeemed with the blood of my Jesus; yet, I confess to Your Paternity the whole truth candidly, I have been exceedingly afflicted — even shedding copious tears before the Crucifix — at seeing the ardent promises, made here by many grave Fathers, to some, of being fully consoled; for I too would fain have now some like consolation; yet I claim it from no other than from the paternal charity of Your Paternity, from whom I instantly ask that you confirm to me the vow which I have made of this with the permission of my Father Rector in this secular year; for I feel myself consumed with the desire of offering my whole self to my Lord, and while it is granted me to fulfill it, I esteem it my greatest consolation to offer myself in vow with such a holocaust; and offering myself to you, I commend myself in the holy sacrifices."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 126,
    "year": 1640,
    "date": "October 14, 1640",
    "place": "Palermo",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 742, 390",
    "excerpt": "The news that comes here of the calling to the Indies of some from the Province of Naples, though on the one hand it has brought me the greatest consolation, as I rejoice in their joy and in the aid they will give to that nascent Church, yet on the other, in not seeing myself yet called, has caused me so great an affliction that I am compelled by grief to inform Your Paternity of it, and to pray you in visceribus Iesu Christi (in the heart of Jesus Christ) to have pity and compassion on me — for you know how ardent and fervent my longing for the Japan mission has ever been.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "The news that comes here of the calling to the Indies of some from the Province of Naples, though on the one hand it has brought me the greatest consolation, as I rejoice in their joy and in the aid they will give to that nascent Church, yet on the other, in not seeing myself yet called, has caused me so great an affliction that I am compelled by grief to inform Your Paternity of it, and to pray you in visceribus Iesu Christi (in the heart of Jesus Christ) to have pity and compassion on me — for you know how ardent and fervent my longing for the Japan mission has ever been. Our Father, the solicitude of this business so consumes me that I know not what more to do, seeing I cannot pray you more effectively than I have always done. For the rest, I place myself wholly in the paternal hands of Your Paternity, who, I know, will not consent that I remain afflicted as I now am; nor do I ask anything else of you but that you give me at least some sure hope for the quiet of my heart — for truly I am much, much troubled. This being for nothing else, I commend myself in the holy sacrifices."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 146,
    "year": 1642,
    "date": "February 7, 1642",
    "place": "Caltanissetta",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 743, 334",
    "excerpt": "It matters greatly to me, for obtaining my longed-for Mission of Japan, or China, to clarify anew and more distinctly to Your Paternity how my vocation to the Indies has always been for Japan, or China, and not for the other parts; because I cannot believe that she, being informed of this truth, would abandon me in the melancholies in which I find myself. I, [Our] Father, conjure her charity (I have no more efficacious words to express my heart to her) to wish to aid a son of hers in Christ, who is bursting with melancholy — but with nothing else than to inform herself of the fact; and if she finds me a liar, I pray her to punish me most severely.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "It matters greatly to me, for obtaining my longed-for Mission of Japan, or China, to clarify anew and more distinctly to Your Paternity how my vocation to the Indies has always been for Japan, or China, and not for the other parts; because I cannot believe that she, being informed of this truth, would abandon me in the melancholies in which I find myself. I, [Our] Father, conjure her charity (I have no more efficacious words to express my heart to her) to wish to aid a son of hers in Christ, who is bursting with melancholy — but with nothing else than to inform herself of the fact; and if she finds me a liar, I pray her to punish me most severely. I go mad thinking that the Lord has mortified me in having offered me the Philippines, for which I have declared I have no vocation; for, this having been pointed out to me by many, to remove the equivocation I have asked explicitly for Japan, or China, and so I have been accustomed to write to the Father Assistant of Portugal, and to Father Alvaro Semedo, Procurator of China; nor have I ever wished to write to the Father Procurator of the Philippines. Very many of the Province bear witness to this, chiefly my Master of Novices and the Masters of philosophy, as in the enclosed Your Paternity will be able to see; the same is seen in the enclosed formula of the [vow], which I offered in the secular year, when — having asked of Your Paternity leave to make a vow of it, and having had no reply on this point — I asked my Father Provincial to grant me license as far as his power could extend; which he doing for my consolation, I made the present formula, which many times a day I am accustomed to renew. Sorrow torments me too much; I do not know how to express my ardor to her — especially Your Paternity having added that she marveled that for the Philippines my sister made a difficulty, and not for Japan. I did not bring forward my sister as a difficulty for the Philippines; but to show Your Paternity that I am more than ever inflamed in my vocation for Japan, or China, I only said that, having had difficulty on account of my sister, yet to go to my longed-for Mission I was cutting away everything; and so de facto (in fact) I was about to dispose of it all, and indeed [it seems] the Lord had concurred abundantly in this, for then a vocation came to my sister to become a nun, and so I easily disentangled myself from everything. And already I was all ready to depart, although [hidden from me] the Call from Your Paternity were for the Philippines, or Japan and China — thinking I, and many others, that one Mission had been mistaken for the other — and in this form I wrote, taking leave of my Master of the Academy; but the Father Provincial having written to me anew that the Call was for the Philippines, [I drew back], and I held back the letter which I was to send to the said Father, which for my consolation Your Paternity will see.",
     "Our Father, have compassion on me, who am dying of pain; I consume myself thinking that the devil, for the punishment of my sins, must triumph [in] a material error — seeing that if Your Paternity changes [it], she will lift me from so many afflictions. I want to speak clearly, for the discharge of my conscience: Our Father, I am called by God ardently; nor do I leave [the scruple] to Your Paternity. Inform yourself well of the fact; and if you find that I am innocent, why will you permit that I lacerate myself with melancholy? I do not speak with exaggeration, for already some [nights] there assaults my heart a most [burning] sorrow, and they prognosticate for me some grave infirmity. I shall always live unhappily — this is [most true]; [and it comes] from poverty of spirit: that if I were well united with the Crucified, I ought to exult seeing myself mortified by God, and by my Superiors, in a thing so much at my heart, and without my fault.",
     "Too much have I wearied Your Paternity; forgive my ardor, and remember that I, with that to which she has done so great a charity — as is the calling me to the Philippines — my heart breaks; I have no words to show her my pain. The Most Blessed Virgin, from whom I know this vocation, will have compassion on me. This I pray Your Paternity: to send back to me the enclosed letters. And I commend myself to her in the Holy Sacrifices."
    ]
   }
  ]
 },
