 {
  "id": "hist-105",
  "fictional": false,
  "name": "Domenico Arganantio",
  "author_raw": "Arganantio, Domenico",
  "age": null,
  "yearsInSociety": null,
  "formation": null,
  "studies": null,
  "skills": [],
  "health": null,
  "assignment": null,
  "destination": "east-indies",
  "dossier": "Wrote 3 letters from Noto (1640) petitioning the General for the foreign missions; requested destinations: East Indies (2), China (1).",
  "letters": [
   {
    "n": 105,
    "year": 1640,
    "date": "January 5, 1640",
    "place": "Noto",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 742, 216",
    "excerpt": "I come with tears in my eyes to prostrate myself at the feet of Your Paternity, to kiss them a thousand and a thousand times with all reverence, as a respectful son desirous of being consoled by his dear father. I return to renew my wonted petition, for I cannot do otherwise, feeling myself continually urged to it by interior impulses. I know not what to do; my heart burns and consumes itself in living flames of desire to suffer for love of our Crucified One. I, Our Father, see very well how unworthy I am of a gift so matchless; but what shall I do, what shall I do?",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "I come with tears in my eyes to prostrate myself at the feet of Your Paternity, to kiss them a thousand and a thousand times with all reverence, as a respectful son desirous of being consoled by his dear father. I return to renew my wonted petition, for I cannot do otherwise, feeling myself continually urged to it by interior impulses. I know not what to do; my heart burns and consumes itself in living flames of desire to suffer for love of our Crucified One. I, Our Father, see very well how unworthy I am of a gift so matchless; but what shall I do, what shall I do? I feel impelled to ask for it, and those words of our Master in the Holy Gospel give me great confidence: petite, et accipietis, pulsate, et aperietur vobis (Ask, and you shall receive; knock, and it shall be opened to you.) Hence I shall never cease knocking until that most happy door is opened to me. Of the qualities required for these enterprises I find none in myself; I can only say that, by the grace of the Lord, I am in very good health; as to the spirit, I trust in the Lord that He will help me with His most holy grace; of the other qualities I know not what to say. I console myself in thinking that He who gave me the vocation will give me, of His infinite goodness, all that is required for it. The sole end of this request is the glory of the Lord, the salvation of my soul, the help of our neighbors, and to suffer what is sufferable for love of our Lord; so that — I am not ashamed to say it again and again — by God's grace I fear nothing for love of my Beloved: neither gallows, nor crosses, nor flames. Hasten, hasten, Our Father, for the love of the Lord and of the Blessed Virgin, console as soon as possible this son of yours, Your Paternity. I close the letter commending myself with all my heart to the holy blessing of Your Paternity and of the whole Society."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 109,
    "year": 1640,
    "date": "February 18, 1640",
    "place": "Noto",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 742, 245",
    "excerpt": "The time so longed for by me has already come, and so ardently asked of God Our Lord with so many prayers: now Your Paternity can satisfy these my kindled desires to suffer something for the love of my Lord. Our Father, let not Your Paternity abandon me in this fine opportunity of these happy tidings that come to us, by the Divine grace, from China. Oh, how my heart has been inflamed with living flames of desire to suffer for the love of my crucified Lord! Our Father, Our Father, with tears in my eyes I supplicate Your Paternity not to forget this your unworthy son.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "The time so longed for by me has already come, and so ardently asked of God Our Lord with so many prayers: now Your Paternity can satisfy these my kindled desires to suffer something for the love of my Lord. Our Father, let not Your Paternity abandon me in this fine opportunity of these happy tidings that come to us, by the Divine grace, from China. Oh, how my heart has been inflamed with living flames of desire to suffer for the love of my crucified Lord! Our Father, Our Father, with tears in my eyes I supplicate Your Paternity not to forget this your unworthy son. Most certain — and God knows how many my desires are — my heart boils as I write this. These are my desires; yet I am wholly conformed to the Will of the Lord, and indifferent to doing or omitting whatever Your Paternity shall judge in Domino (in the Lord) to be the greater glory of the Lord and the salvation of my soul."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 115,
    "year": 1640,
    "date": "May 17, 1640",
    "place": "Noto",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 742, 281",
    "excerpt": "I received Your Paternity's letter of the 5th of April, which filled my soul to the brim with unspeakable joy, to see that the memory of me, a most vile sinner, is so alive before him. Truly all of this must be attributed to the great charity of so loving a Father, and not to the merits of so miserable a son, and unworthy of every good. Confiding therefore in the great benignity of Your Paternity, I do not weary of multiplying letters to lay open the ardent and kindled desires of my heart concerning the mission of the Indies; nor does the lack of an occasion at present hinder me at all in my wonted desire — rather, it goes on being fomented with still greater increase, for it is most true what is commonly said, that privation most often generates a greater appetite for things.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "I received Your Paternity's letter of the 5th of April, which filled my soul to the brim with unspeakable joy, to see that the memory of me, a most vile sinner, is so alive before him. Truly all of this must be attributed to the great charity of so loving a Father, and not to the merits of so miserable a son, and unworthy of every good. Confiding therefore in the great benignity of Your Paternity, I do not weary of multiplying letters to lay open the ardent and kindled desires of my heart concerning the mission of the Indies; nor does the lack of an occasion at present hinder me at all in my wonted desire — rather, it goes on being fomented with still greater increase, for it is most true what is commonly said, that privation most often generates a greater appetite for things. For the rest, I will not multiply words further, for Your Paternity now possesses entirely the whole interior of my heart. In the meantime I shall attend, as Your Paternity exhorts me, to making continual prayer to God our Lord concerning this matter, that He would direct the affair to His greater glory, the good of my soul, and the salvation of our neighbors — for all this is my end, nor do I pretend to anything else in this petition; and I stand awaiting from the hand of God whatever shall be determined concerning it, conforming myself in all things to the divine good pleasure. Finally, our Father, I, as a most unworthy son, most humbly bow and ask from Your Paternity his holy blessing, commending myself with all my heart to his most holy Sacrifices and devout Prayers."
    ]
   }
  ]
 },
