  "dossier": "Wrote 2 letters from Naples, San [Solio] (1640) petitioning the General for the foreign missions; requested destination: East Indies (2).",
  "letters": [
   {
    "n": 108,
    "year": 1640,
    "date": "February 4, 1640",
    "place": "Naples",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 742, 237",
    "excerpt": "The offering of the Holy Child Jesus to His eternal Father in the Temple — whose feast we celebrated the day before yesterday — and that which, tomorrow being so many years since, our three holy Martyrs made to the same of their lives and blood, have moved me to renew in turn the offering that I have made at other times to the same Holy Child: of spending in His holy Service in the Indies the few days and few forces that remain to me in this miserable life, helping to cultivate that harvest, watered, whatever other cause there might be, with the Precious Blood of the blessed Christ; and to pray Your Paternity to deign to grant me this grace, which, as I have at other times hinted to Your Paternity, I esteem the greatest that I can receive in this life, after that which I hope for as the end of this one: holy Martyrdom.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "The offering of the Holy Child Jesus to His eternal Father in the Temple — whose feast we celebrated the day before yesterday — and that which, tomorrow being so many years since, our three holy Martyrs made to the same of their lives and blood, have moved me to renew in turn the offering that I have made at other times to the same Holy Child: of spending in His holy Service in the Indies the few days and few forces that remain to me in this miserable life, helping to cultivate that harvest, watered, whatever other cause there might be, with the Precious Blood of the blessed Christ; and to pray Your Paternity to deign to grant me this grace, which, as I have at other times hinted to Your Paternity, I esteem the greatest that I can receive in this life, after that which I hope for as the end of this one: holy Martyrdom. My Father General, do not, for the love of blessed God, regard my inabilities and imperfections, but only the dignity and worth of the personage who has been pleased to give me such a vocation — who, I hold evidently, is blessed God. And I promise him now, provided he grant me such a grace, to offer for Your Paternity a great part of what His Divine Majesty shall deign to let me suffer there. Wherewith, in the end making to Your Paternity most humble reverence, I pray you will give me your holy blessing."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 124,
    "year": 1640,
    "date": "September 29, 1640",
    "place": "San [Solio]",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 742, 382",
    "excerpt": "This very morning I heard here at San [Solio], where I find myself for the vacation, that Your Paternity has already sent to some the most happy news for the Indies — among whom I have not seen myself numbered. I confess that I am the most unworthy of all, which I say most sincerely; but I pray Your Paternity that if others of this Province are to be sent — which I hope without doubt — she send among them also me, poor wretch, so that I may be made worthy to spend the little life that remains to me in the service of that Lord whom I have so many times offended.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "This very morning I heard here at San [Solio], where I find myself for the vacation, that Your Paternity has already sent to some the most happy news for the Indies — among whom I have not seen myself numbered. I confess that I am the most unworthy of all, which I say most sincerely; but I pray Your Paternity that if others of this Province are to be sent — which I hope without doubt — she send among them also me, poor wretch, so that I may be made worthy to spend the little life that remains to me in the service of that Lord whom I have so many times offended. Our Father, I recognize myself bound to blessed God in extraordinary measure, and perhaps more than any other, for the great perils from which He has delivered me; and precisely today, the glorious day of Saint Michael the Archangel, twelve years ago, He delivered me from a perilous accident of my life — for, tumbling from a steep rock some fifty-seven palms above the ground, I received no injury from the fall, though I ought to have perished, as those who saw the precipice judged before I reached the ground. So let Your Paternity be content, for the love of blessed God, that I may spend these few days, which in His supreme goodness He has deigned to prolong for me, there in the Indies in aid of those poor souls, bereft of the light of the holy faith. The Father Provincial long ago promised me that he would not hinder me on account of the school, about which he had informed me. So I shall await this good news from Your Paternity, and I shall pray blessed God in the Spiritual Exercises, which I am about to begin as soon as possible, to deign to inspire Your Paternity to grant it to me. And with this, making to Your Paternity most humble reverence, I pray you give me your holy blessing."
    ]
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "id": "hist-110",
  "fictional": false,
  "name": "Lorenzo Arisco",
  "author_raw": "Arisco, Lorenzo",
  "age": null,
  "yearsInSociety": null,
