  "skills": [],
  "health": null,
  "assignment": null,
  "destination": "east-indies",
  "dossier": "Wrote 3 letters from Caltagirone (1640) petitioning the General for the foreign missions; requested destination: East Indies (3).",
  "letters": [
   {
    "n": 110,
    "year": 1640,
    "date": "February 22, 1640",
    "place": "Caltagirone",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 742, 247",
    "excerpt": "Ever since my novitiate God Our Lord has given me a lively desire to ask for the Mission of the Indies; but because I was always told that such a thing was impossible to obtain for us brothers, I never proposed it to Your Paternity. Now, hearing that a great number of laborers is being sent to those parts, my longing has been the more kindled; wherefore, having communicated it to the Father Provincial, he tells me to take it up with Your Paternity and that he will not fail on his part to help me in what he can.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "Ever since my novitiate God Our Lord has given me a lively desire to ask for the Mission of the Indies; but because I was always told that such a thing was impossible to obtain for us brothers, I never proposed it to Your Paternity. Now, hearing that a great number of laborers is being sent to those parts, my longing has been the more kindled; wherefore, having communicated it to the Father Provincial, he tells me to take it up with Your Paternity and that he will not fail on his part to help me in what he can. Now I, Our Father, have recourse to Your Paternity, praying you with the greatest affection and efficacy possible to me, within the bounds of the indifference that holy Obedience requires of us, to console me also by placing me among the first who shall embark for those countries. The number of the Fathers, as I understand, is very great, whence it cannot be but that they still have need of some share of brothers. I am young in age and of very good strength; by profession a tailor, though I have done and offer to do each and every office wherein the superiors may wish to employ me. I have thought and thought again of how much adversity and travail could frighten me in such an enterprise, and against them all divine mercy gives me courage. So there remains nothing but to propose to Your Paternity, as I do with this present letter, this my Vocation, praying His Divine Majesty to deign to inspire you with what shall be in this matter His greater glory. Wherewith I commend myself greatly to the most holy sacrifices and prayers of Your Paternity."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 120,
    "year": 1640,
    "date": "July 13, 1640",
    "place": "Caltagirone",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 742, 336",
    "excerpt": "On the first of last Lent I wrote to Your Paternity a letter in which I disclosed to him the great desire which God our Lord has deigned to give me ever since my novitiate, of serving Him in the Indies, offering myself to Your Paternity — that if he had need there of coadjutor brothers, I, who by the grace of the Lord am young and have strength, was most ready to go thither.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "On the first of last Lent I wrote to Your Paternity a letter in which I disclosed to him the great desire which God our Lord has deigned to give me ever since my novitiate, of serving Him in the Indies, offering myself to Your Paternity — that if he had need there of coadjutor brothers, I, who by the grace of the Lord am young and have strength, was most ready to go thither. With this I again beseech Your Paternity, for the love he may have for Jesus Christ, that he would grant my petition, and do me the charity of sending me to those parts as soon as possible; for as for me, on the one hand I am indifferent to whatever exercise the superiors there shall apply me to, be it two or three; on the other hand, here I have no attachment to anything that might impede my departure — rather, I shall receive the notice to depart, when Your Paternity shall do me the charity of calling me, as if it were an angel calling me to the glory of Paradise, for to labor for the love of God and to serve my Fathers, who aid those poor souls redeemed with the most precious blood of the Son of God, is to me a Paradise, and I do not hold it inferior to glory — which I pray God our Lord to grant to Your Paternity, to me, to all of our Holy Company, and to all the world. With which I commend myself to his holy sacrifices and prayers."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 127,
    "year": 1640,
    "date": "October 28, 1640",
    "place": "Caltagirone",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 742, 394",
    "excerpt": "Seeing that from our College there departs for the Indies, at Your Paternity’s command, Giuseppe Plazza, and hearing that many will go, has kindled a flame in my heart that will not let me rest. It is true that I am a poor brother; but I was healed of a malignant fever by a saint whom I did not know at the time and of whom I had never heard word — and then, having entered the Company, I came to know that the saint I saw in a dream was Saint Xavier. Now he calls me to the Indies, and I give notice of it to Your Paternity and supplicate you, as I have done at other times.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "Seeing that from our College there departs for the Indies, at Your Paternity’s command, Giuseppe Plazza, and hearing that many will go, has kindled a flame in my heart that will not let me rest. It is true that I am a poor brother; but I was healed of a malignant fever by a saint whom I did not know at the time and of whom I had never heard word — and then, having entered the Company, I came to know that the saint I saw in a dream was Saint Xavier. Now he calls me to the Indies, and I give notice of it to Your Paternity and supplicate you, as I have done at other times. Here I cannot labor as I would, for though I do all the offices and services as I know how and can — and have often done, amid the murmurings of the brothers — the superiors restrain me; but in the Indies one man will need to do all things. If there be difficulty about the expense, I will bear it at least in part. For the love of God and of Saint Xavier, who healed me and made me enter the Company, let Your Paternity console me, and at once; and afterward I shall report to Your Paternity all that I shall suffer and do in the Indies, for the service of God and of the Fathers and brothers; at the most holy sacrifices and prayers I commend myself to Your Paternity."
    ]
   }
  ]
 },
 {
